{{also|desiderátum}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|la|dēsīderātum||something that is desired}}, {{glossary|neuter}} {{glossary|nominative}} {{glossary|singular}} of {{m|la|dēsīderātus}}, the {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|past}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|dēsīderāre||to desire}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|part of speech=n|id=50832|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref> from {{m|la|dēsīderō||to desire, want, wish for}}, from {{m|la|de-||''intensifying prefix''}} + possibly {{m|la|sīdus||star; constellation}} though the connection is unclear. The English word is cognate with {{cog|fr|désideratum}}, {{cog|es|desiderátum}}.

The plural is derived from {{der|en|la|dēsīderāta}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/dɪˌsɪdəˈɹɑːtəm/|/-ˌzɪ-/|/-ˈɹeɪ-/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/dəˌsɪdəˈɹɑtəm/|/-ˌzɪ-/|/-ɾəm/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|de|si|de|ra|tum|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|desiderata}}

# Something that is [[wish#Verb|wished]] for, or considered [[desirable]]. {{defdate|from mid 17th c.}}
#* '''1907''', ''The Medical Brief'' (volume 35, page 840)
#*: A heavy clay soil is bad for all neuralgics, and the house should be dry, and on a sandy or gravel soil. The '''desideratum''' for all neuralgic affections is perpetual summer {{...}}

